# Akasha ontology — Philosophy World: the author spine (the thinkers a genealogy needs).
# Extends thinkers.ak with the philosophers the 17 notions lean on, across the three eras of
# the Baccalauréat author list. Each carries a wp:<Title> Wikipedia exit. Historical figures
# only; summaries are compact and factual. Links to base phil:field:* concepts.

# ── Antiquity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def "phil:thinker:epicurus" "Epicurus (341–270 BCE) — Athenian founder of Epicureanism. Happiness is ataraxia: the absence of bodily pain and mental disturbance, reached by simple pleasures, friendship and freedom from the fear of death and the gods."
al phil:thinker:epicurus epicurus
al phil:thinker:epicurus wp:Epicurus
ln phil:thinker:epicurus phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:epicurus phil:field:ethics thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:epicurus"

def "phil:thinker:augustine" "Augustine of Hippo (354–430) — North African bishop and Church Father who fused Christian faith with Platonism. In the Confessions he analysed time as a stretching of the soul (memory, attention, expectation), and wrestled with will, grace and evil."
al phil:thinker:augustine augustine
al phil:thinker:augustine wp:Augustine_of_Hippo
ln phil:thinker:augustine phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:augustine phil:field:metaphysics thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:augustine"

# ── Modern ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def "phil:thinker:hobbes" "Thomas Hobbes (1588–1679) — English political philosopher. In Leviathan he derived the sovereign state from a social covenant by which individuals escape the 'war of all against all' of the state of nature."
al phil:thinker:hobbes hobbes
al phil:thinker:hobbes wp:Thomas_Hobbes
ln phil:thinker:hobbes phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:hobbes phil:field:political_philosophy thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:hobbes"

def "phil:thinker:spinoza" "Baruch Spinoza (1632–1677) — Dutch rationalist. In the Ethics, God and Nature are one substance; there is no free will, and freedom is acting from the necessity of one's own nature, guided by reason toward joy."
al phil:thinker:spinoza spinoza
al phil:thinker:spinoza wp:Baruch_Spinoza
ln phil:thinker:spinoza phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:spinoza phil:rationalism thesaurus:related
ln phil:thinker:spinoza phil:field:metaphysics thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:spinoza"

def "phil:thinker:locke" "John Locke (1632–1704) — English empiricist and political thinker. The mind is a blank slate written by experience; government rests on the consent of the governed and the protection of natural rights; property is founded on one's own labour."
al phil:thinker:locke locke
al phil:thinker:locke wp:John_Locke
ln phil:thinker:locke phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:locke phil:empiricism thesaurus:related
ln phil:thinker:locke phil:field:political_philosophy thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:locke"

def "phil:thinker:hume" "David Hume (1711–1776) — Scottish empiricist and sceptic. He argued that all ideas derive from impressions, that causation is a habit of the mind, that induction cannot be rationally justified, and that reason is 'the slave of the passions'."
al phil:thinker:hume hume
al phil:thinker:hume wp:David_Hume
ln phil:thinker:hume phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:hume phil:empiricism thesaurus:related
ln phil:thinker:hume phil:field:epistemology thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:hume"

def "phil:thinker:rousseau" "Jean-Jacques Rousseau (1712–1778) — Genevan philosopher of the Enlightenment. Humans are good by nature and corrupted by society; legitimate authority rests on the general will, by which a people gives itself its own law."
al phil:thinker:rousseau rousseau
al phil:thinker:rousseau wp:Jean-Jacques_Rousseau
ln phil:thinker:rousseau phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:rousseau phil:field:political_philosophy thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:rousseau"

# ── Contemporary (19th–20th c., historical) ──────────────────────────────────
def "phil:thinker:hegel" "Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel (1770–1831) — German idealist. History and mind advance dialectically toward the self-knowledge of Spirit; in the master–slave dialectic, self-consciousness is won through labour and the struggle for recognition."
al phil:thinker:hegel hegel
al phil:thinker:hegel wp:Georg_Wilhelm_Friedrich_Hegel
ln phil:thinker:hegel phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:hegel philosophy.dialectics thesaurus:related
ln phil:thinker:hegel phil:field:metaphysics thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:hegel"

def "phil:thinker:mill" "John Stuart Mill (1806–1873) — English utilitarian and liberal. Actions are right as they promote happiness; he refined utility with higher and lower pleasures, and defended individual liberty under the harm principle."
al phil:thinker:mill mill
al phil:thinker:mill wp:John_Stuart_Mill
ln phil:thinker:mill phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:mill phil:utilitarianism thesaurus:related
ln phil:thinker:mill phil:field:ethics thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:mill"

def "phil:thinker:marx" "Karl Marx (1818–1883) — German philosopher, economist and revolutionary. He analysed capitalism through alienated labour and class struggle, and held that the material conditions of production shape consciousness, law and the state."
al phil:thinker:marx marx
al phil:thinker:marx wp:Karl_Marx
ln phil:thinker:marx phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:marx phil:field:political_philosophy thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:marx"

def "phil:thinker:freud" "Sigmund Freud (1856–1939) — Austrian founder of psychoanalysis. He placed an unconscious of repressed desire at the centre of mental life, read through dreams, slips and symptoms, and modelled the psyche as id, ego and superego."
al phil:thinker:freud freud
al phil:thinker:freud wp:Sigmund_Freud
ln phil:thinker:freud phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:freud phil:field:philosophy_of_mind thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:freud"

def "phil:thinker:bergson" "Henri Bergson (1859–1941) — French philosopher. He opposed lived duration (durée) to the spatialised time of clocks and science, and distinguished a closed morality of social pressure from an open morality of aspiration."
al phil:thinker:bergson bergson
al phil:thinker:bergson wp:Henri_Bergson
ln phil:thinker:bergson phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:bergson phil:field:metaphysics thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:bergson"

def "phil:thinker:sartre" "Jean-Paul Sartre (1905–1980) — French existentialist. Existence precedes essence: with no given nature, the human is 'condemned to be free' and wholly responsible; he criticised the Freudian unconscious as bad faith."
al phil:thinker:sartre sartre
al phil:thinker:sartre wp:Jean-Paul_Sartre
ln phil:thinker:sartre phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:sartre phil:existentialism thesaurus:related
ln phil:thinker:sartre phil:field:ethics thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:sartre"

def "phil:thinker:arendt" "Hannah Arendt (1906–1975) — German-American political theorist. She distinguished labour (meeting life's necessity), work (making durable things) and action (appearing among others in speech and deed), and analysed power, freedom and the banality of evil."
al phil:thinker:arendt arendt
al phil:thinker:arendt wp:Hannah_Arendt
ln phil:thinker:arendt phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:arendt phil:field:political_philosophy thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:arendt"

# ── Further spine: modern & 20th-century (all historical) ────────────────────
def "phil:thinker:bacon" "Francis Bacon (1561–1626) — English statesman and philosopher of science. He attacked the 'idols' that mislead the mind and championed inductive, experimental method: knowledge drawn from nature to give humanity power over it."
al phil:thinker:bacon bacon
al phil:thinker:bacon wp:Francis_Bacon
ln phil:thinker:bacon phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:bacon phil:empiricism thesaurus:related
ln phil:thinker:bacon phil:field:philosophy_of_science thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:bacon"

def "phil:thinker:pascal" "Blaise Pascal (1623–1662) — French mathematician and religious thinker. He weighed the 'reasons of the heart' against pure reason, framed the wager on faith, and described the human condition suspended between infinity and nothingness."
al phil:thinker:pascal pascal
al phil:thinker:pascal wp:Blaise_Pascal
ln phil:thinker:pascal phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:pascal phil:field:metaphysics thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:pascal"

def "phil:thinker:leibniz" "Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz (1646–1716) — German rationalist and polymath. He held that the world is composed of simple substances (monads), that this is the best of all possible worlds, and that nothing is without a sufficient reason."
al phil:thinker:leibniz leibniz
al phil:thinker:leibniz wp:Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz
ln phil:thinker:leibniz phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:leibniz phil:rationalism thesaurus:related
ln phil:thinker:leibniz phil:field:metaphysics thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:leibniz"

def "phil:thinker:schopenhauer" "Arthur Schopenhauer (1788–1860) — German philosopher. He read the world as blind, striving Will beneath appearance, the source of suffering, from which art and ascetic renunciation offer relief."
al phil:thinker:schopenhauer schopenhauer
al phil:thinker:schopenhauer wp:Arthur_Schopenhauer
ln phil:thinker:schopenhauer phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:schopenhauer phil:field:metaphysics thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:schopenhauer"

def "phil:thinker:husserl" "Edmund Husserl (1859–1938) — German founder of phenomenology. He sought to return 'to the things themselves' by describing the structures of consciousness, which is always consciousness of something (intentionality)."
al phil:thinker:husserl husserl
al phil:thinker:husserl wp:Edmund_Husserl
ln phil:thinker:husserl phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:husserl phil:field:phenomenology thesaurus:related
ln phil:thinker:husserl phil:field:philosophy_of_mind thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:husserl"

def "phil:thinker:heidegger" "Martin Heidegger (1889–1976) — German philosopher. In Being and Time he reopened the question of Being through the analysis of human existence (Dasein) as being-in-the-world and being-toward-death, and later warned that technology reduces the world to standing resource."
al phil:thinker:heidegger heidegger
al phil:thinker:heidegger wp:Martin_Heidegger
ln phil:thinker:heidegger phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:heidegger phil:field:phenomenology thesaurus:related
ln phil:thinker:heidegger phil:field:metaphysics thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:heidegger"

def "phil:thinker:wittgenstein" "Ludwig Wittgenstein (1889–1951) — Austrian-British philosopher. The early Tractatus pictured language as mirroring the world and drew the limits of what can be said; the later work tied meaning to use in 'language-games' woven into forms of life."
al phil:thinker:wittgenstein wittgenstein
al phil:thinker:wittgenstein wp:Ludwig_Wittgenstein
ln phil:thinker:wittgenstein phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:wittgenstein phil:field:logic thesaurus:related
ln phil:thinker:wittgenstein phil:field:epistemology thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:wittgenstein"

def "phil:thinker:merleau_ponty" "Maurice Merleau-Ponty (1908–1961) — French phenomenologist. He placed the lived, perceiving body at the centre of experience, arguing that consciousness is embodied and that perception, not pure intellect, is our first opening onto the world."
al phil:thinker:merleau_ponty merleau_ponty
al phil:thinker:merleau_ponty wp:Maurice_Merleau-Ponty
ln phil:thinker:merleau_ponty phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:merleau_ponty phil:field:phenomenology thesaurus:related
ln phil:thinker:merleau_ponty phil:field:philosophy_of_mind thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:merleau_ponty"

def "phil:thinker:beauvoir" "Simone de Beauvoir (1908–1986) — French existentialist philosopher and writer. In The Second Sex she analysed how 'one is not born, but becomes, a woman', founding modern feminist philosophy on freedom, situation and the Other."
al phil:thinker:beauvoir beauvoir
al phil:thinker:beauvoir wp:Simone_de_Beauvoir
ln phil:thinker:beauvoir phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:beauvoir phil:existentialism thesaurus:related
ln phil:thinker:beauvoir phil:field:ethics thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:beauvoir"

def "phil:thinker:foucault" "Michel Foucault (1926–1984) — French philosopher and historian of thought. He studied how knowledge and power shape the subject through history — in madness, discipline, punishment and sexuality — showing that what counts as truth has a genealogy."
al phil:thinker:foucault foucault
al phil:thinker:foucault wp:Michel_Foucault
ln phil:thinker:foucault phil:philosophy sys:part_of
ln phil:thinker:foucault phil:field:political_philosophy thesaurus:related
set.add name="phil:thinkers" id="phil:thinker:foucault"
